A||||20||LPR||Really Stupid Question.....||||3||
Z||000000||LPR||04-25-2001||10:21 AM||||I've given this a lot of thought so be nice...<BR>Is it at all possible to convert the standard hydraulic clutch system to a cable run system?  I'm sick of not being able to adjust the stupid thing, and love the feel of my Volkswagon's clutch!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">I realize that you would have to get a long and strong cable make special mounts and everything.... but is there a slight chance it may just work?  Is there too much force for a cable?<BR>Thanks <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Luc||||reg||3
Z||000001||Cheever2||04-25-2001||11:59 AM||||I once had a Baja Bug with a stonger clutch than my GT.  If you can install it, there should be no reason it wouldn't work.<P>My stupid question would be if you could put a handle on the shifter instead of using a pedal.  I'm talking about something like a bicycle brake handle.  Your hand is on the shifter <I>anyway</I>, so why not just squeeze, shift, and release?  This would be for the hydraulic clutches, not for your cable idea.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000002||84Bill||04-25-2001||11:59 AM||||Very interesting question. The actuator on the trans will have to be replace with something of your own design. As far a the cable is conserned you could probably get away with a th125 shift cable. How would you rig up a peddal... hydraulic sounds like the better deal after considering all this||||reg||1
Z||000003||LPR||04-25-2001||12:08 PM||||So this really might work! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I don't care about expenses or labor yet, it would be fun toying around with petal design and actuators!  I have access to a metal shop so I could toy with this project!  I just might try it, unless there is anything else I am failing to consider.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR>Luc||||reg||4
Z||000004||thomas_l||04-25-2001||01:17 PM||||While in the junkyard fishing for odds-n-ends I noticed a number of early 80's Cavaliers/Citations that had cable pulls on the clutch. I suppose you could use the clutch arm and rig something up or just use the whole tranny. Sounds like an awful lot of work tho...||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||04-25-2001||03:06 PM||||I hate it when you guys go making me think - !  At this point, I'm thinking about the "handlegrip-on-shifter clutch," using a motorcycle's (hydraulic) master cylinder.<P>...but, I'm not thinking <B>real</B> hard, then, either   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>BTW, my 1982 Cavalier had a self-adjusting, cable-operated clutch.  Worked just fine - through at least 1.5 (?) clutch lifetimes.  The front engine, FWD configuration was great for this - the clutch cable had an almost straight route.  On midengine, RWD, limited space car like Fiero, though, sounds like it could be a challenge.<P>FWIW, my Corvair had an excellent, smooth, cable-operated clutch.  It used sectors of nylon pulleys mounted in 2 places on the floor, an overcenter spring on the pedal, a greaseable cross-shaft w/zerk fitting, and a hardened, threaded push rod to operate the fork.  No, I'm not kidding.  In other words, if you go to a lot of trouble, you can make it work great.<P>...And,just to complicate things, on the Corvair, the parking brake cable shared the same routing and pulley mounts, as the clutch cable!  Pretty slick design, I thought.<p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000006||LPR||04-25-2001||04:13 PM||||That does sound like a pretty slick idea!  I'm not that slick though, I like the cable idea... umm... easier<P>I went out and looked at my car. It seems like it wouldn't be too hard to mount a cable pulling away from the driver towards the front of the car with the clutch lever.  Once installed like that it would be almost a clear shot up the console to the rear of the car were it would meet up with a fabricated clamp that would pull a fabricated actuator towards the passenger side of the car.<P>This seems like the best solution, I think.  This way there wouldn't be many turns in the cable and it would be the minimum length, I could use a shifter cable!<P>Let me know what you think!  Oh, I also am wondering how much force it takes to release the clutch, seems like a lot!??  I need to know so I can calculate the forces on the parts I *may* fabricate(salvage).<P>Thanks, Luc<p>[This message has been edited by LPR (edited 04-25-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||04-25-2001||04:27 PM||||I am picturing the cluch cable setup on an air cooled VW, the pedal was about a foot, and the cable hook was about an inch 1/2. so the gain was about 8.<P>  stepping on the pedal?? 50lbs? maybe?<P>  so you are looking at about 400 lbs of tension on the clutch cable. Dont forget you will need a spring retractor so the throwout bearing doesnt ride against the clutch plate all the time.<P>   the hydro clutch on a fiero cannot be adjusted at all?! What happens when the cluch wears?<P>  Pesonally I think you are crazy to want to go backwards to a cable. If you are in a creative mood, find a way to make the hydro clutch adjustable. Change the way it interacts with the clutch/tranny so it can be adjusted. I think you will be happier with that in the end.<P>  If you really hate pressing the clutch pedal, remember that you only really need it in first gear. with a little practice you can upshift easily in the rest of the gears without the clutch. Use to do it all the time in my aircooled VWs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Taijiguy||04-25-2001||04:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Posted by LPR <B>Oh, I also am wondering how much force it takes to release the clutch, seems like a lot!?? I need to know so I can calculate the forces on the parts I *may* fabricate(salvage).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>That would be strictly dependant on the force of the pressure plate, and the mechanical advantage of the lever used to compress the springs, and other components of the system. For example, if it would take 300# at the throwout bearing to compress the plate 1", and say the arm on the throwout bearing is 2" long from it's pivot point, and the end that the cable is attached to is 4" long, the force required would be 150#, since there's a mechanical advantage of 2:1, of course, the 4" arm travels 2" to achieve the 1" throw at the bearing, since now the travel ratio is 1:2. You can gain or lose more advantage at the pedal, but you always exchange amount of movement for power applied. These numbers also assume no loss for friction or other normal elements, and are perfect world examples.||||reg||1
Z||000009||LPR||04-25-2001||04:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>the hydro clutch on a fiero cannot be adjusted at all?! What happens when the cluch wears?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The clutch is "self" adjusting, but doesn't allow you to adjust it the way you want it.  Although it may seem stone age to regress to the cable route I feel that it eliminates quite a few of the problems that the hydraulic system has.  <BR>Simple is good, I think this would be simple.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I have driven my standards only using the clutch sometimes, often I just drop it in when the RPM is right. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I like it! ...But you still have to use the clutch, especially for stop and go traffic.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||04-25-2001||04:52 PM||||Stop and go traffic is what makes the hyrdralic clutch soooo nice, it takes about half the force to push compaired to a cable.<P>and remember, the cable for a fiero clutch will have to make a 90° curve back there to get the force in the right direction. The cable on your baha bug ( <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> were a straight line (almost).||||reg||1
Z||000011||LPR||04-25-2001||05:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B> You can gain or lose more advantage at the pedal, but you always exchange amount of movement for power applied.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm a physics major in college and am taking all of this into account while I'm planning. (hey, I'm paying all this money for a reason! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>I could determine how much force was needed to release the clutch with the current arm, but that would be hard!  Does anyone know off-hand how much force is required?||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-25-2001||08:06 PM||||Posible but allot more dificult than you might think.....<P>1. Clutch cables only tolerate a certain amount of curve radius before they bind.<P>2. The more bends involved the more #1 matters.<P>3. The pedal is built to push an object. You'd have to make it pull on a cable. <P>An automatic shifter cable is too short. At least all the car ones you can find likely will be....||||reg||1
Z||000013||LPR||04-25-2001||09:57 PM||||Thanks for all the help everyone!  Is anyone else interested in this or am I just weird?  I personally like the idea... put hey I'm one person, what difference can I possible make. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000014||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-25-2001||10:05 PM||||You're just weird. The slave cylinder can be adjusted in and out.  So, I am confused as to what adjustment you are hoping to make.<P>Hydrallic clutches are more robust and reliable than cable actuated clutches.  For example, a hydrallic clutch actuator is far less likely to sieze in place on you.  They don't squeak and they feel much better.<P>It would be cheaper and easier to pay an experienced Fiero mechanic to fix your car than to "rig-up" a cable clutch.  <P>That's my opinion.  In the end, you will have to make your own chioce.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000015||LPR||04-25-2001||10:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>The slave cylinder can be adjusted in and out</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>So how do you make that adjustment?  Mine is really messed up, at the end of its reach....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-26-2001||03:37 AM||||The slave cylinder is mounted on a bracket which is attached to the transmission.  If you loosen the two bolts, It will slide back and forth to adjust the throw of the slave cylinder.<P>If you have already tried this and got nowhere, then as you have suggested something is not right in "clutch-ville."<P>I drive an automatic Fiero, so I am not going to claim any expertise here.  It is possible that the master, slave, or actuator arm are on the verge of failure/worn out.  The actuator has a bearing (I've seen the part listed on a couple of sites) if this were bad, it might reduce the effectiveness of the slave cylinder too.<P>It's troubleshootin' time.  This is why I suggested going to a trustworthy and experienced mechanic.  They will save you a lot of frustration, maybe some money too.  With any luck, your problem can be solved without pulling the engine.<P>Good luck . . .||||reg||1
Z||000017||Rex||04-26-2001||07:09 PM||||I like the adjustable cable idea.The key would be the distance the throw out bearing is required to travel to disengage the clutch.The ratio could then be determined used on the bell crank that would be fabricated at the end of the clutch. Good luck and keep us posted.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Fierowrecker||04-26-2001||09:16 PM||||Hey Luc!<BR>I have mentioned this before to other people...<BR>Aircraft use a special morse cable that can push as well as pull...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000019||LPR||04-26-2001||10:03 PM||||Wow, all the Idea's and possibilities! This is great! Thanks for all the feed-back.<P>I'll check it out some more this weekend and keep you posted!<P>Luc  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000020||Pontiaddict||04-27-2001||02:54 PM||||Strangely, neither of the Fieros I've owned had clutch actuation problems. I've always had problems with the shift cables. Maybe I should figure out how to make the shifter hydraulic.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>You might be able to use a short cable from a FWD car and a longer cable from a Fiero then use a bellcrank between the two to connect them, change direction, and get the leverage you might need.||||reg||1
